 ## Background
-EWA Splatting was introduced by Matthias Zwicker, Hanspeter Pfister, Jeroen van Baar, and Markus Gross in 2001 [S8, unofficial]. It is named after Heckbert's EWA filter for texture mapping due to the similarity in derivation [S9, abstract only, unofficial]. The method was developed to address aliasing artifacts in volume splatting caused by incorrect visibility determination and non-overlapping reconstruction kernel assumptions [S1, S8, unofficial]. It has been widely used in high-quality volume visualization and has inspired many variants and optimizations, including adaptive EWA splatting and GPU-accelerated implementations [S6, unofficial][S13].
